# CHAIN-LINK corpus · CONTROL ROW, and it is honest about being one.
#
# CELL SAMPLED HERE: the same `<method>().is_none()` shape over a BUILTIN
# receiver (`Vector.pop()`) rather than a user-defined one. This cell was
# GREEN before the chain-link fix and is GREEN after it, so -- unlike its six
# siblings -- it CANNOT be RED-verified against the pre-fix compiler
# (Core #12). Recording that explicitly instead of letting the name imply
# coverage it does not have.
#
# WHAT IT PINS INSTEAD, and why it is worth a row (Core #15e Q6 -- "is this
# passing case ACCIDENTALLY correct?"): it is. Nothing records
# `expr_types[span.start]` for a builtin-receiver chain either, so the outer
# link falls through to `infer_method_return_type` and happens to land on
# `bool`. The green is a property of a DIFFERENT code path, not evidence that
# the chain-link path works. This row pins that path against a future fix in
# this area that "corrects" the builtin route into the contended-slot route --
# i.e. it is the guard against the class being pushed sideways rather than
# retired.
void main():
    Vector[int] empty = Vector[int]()
    print(empty.pop().is_none())
    print(empty.pop().is_some())
    Vector[int] full = Vector[int]()
    full.push(2)
    print(full.pop().is_some())
    print(full.pop().is_none())
